#!/usr/bin/python3
"""disjorn-apps-launch — the ONE privileged step of an apps-builder turn.

INSTALLED AT /usr/local/lib/disjorn/disjorn-apps-launch, root:root 0755, and
named EXACTLY by /etc/sudoers.d/92-disjorn-apps. Sibling of, and modelled line
for line on its elder brother `harness/broker/disjorn-build-launch` (WP-L4) — read that file's header first; the reasoning
about sudoers wildcards, fixed argv and "the validation below is the actual
boundary" is identical here and is not repeated in full.

    disjorn-apps-launch run     <resident> <session> <turn> <app-id> <prompt-path>
    disjorn-apps-launch stop    <resident> <session> <turn>
    disjorn-apps-launch publish <app-id>
    disjorn-apps-launch revert  <app-id>
    disjorn-apps-launch remix   <parent-id> <child-id>

`run` execs, with stdin/stdout/stderr passed through untouched (`--pipe`):

    systemd-run --unit=disjorn-apps-<session>-<turn>
                --uid=res-appsbuilding --gid=res-appsbuilding
                --collect --pipe <fixed limits...> <--setenv=...> --
                /usr/local/lib/disjorn/run-apps.sh <session> <turn> <app-id>
                                                   <prompt-path>

WHY A HELPER AND NOT A SUDOERS LINE FOR systemd-run: sudoers matches command
arguments as ONE concatenated string, so any rule permissive enough to carry a
real systemd-run invocation must end in a wildcard, and a wildcard matches
spaces — which makes it a grant of `--uid=0 --property=ExecStartPre=...`, i.e.
full root. The privileged surface has to be a fixed-argv program that validates
its own inputs. This is that program. The regexes in the sudoers file are a
second fence, not the fence.

`stop` (slice (iv), SPECS/2026-09-08-apps-stop-turn.md) drops a `stop-requested`
marker in the turn's result dir and execs `systemctl stop disjorn-apps-<session>-
<turn>`. The marker is how the harvest tells a user's stop from the clock: both
arrive as the same SIGTERM. Fixed argv, same charsets as `run`, and the unit
name is derived — a caller can name a turn, never a unit.

THE MARKER IS A LABEL AND MUST NEVER PREVENT THE STOP (Claudette #2447). The
turn's result dir is made by run-apps.sh INSIDE the unit, after systemd has
started it, so a stop pressed in the first moments of a turn finds no dir yet.
A marker that cannot be written is reported on stderr and the stop is execed
anyway; the cost is one wrong word in the room ("timed out" for a turn the
user ended) and never a turn that ran on to its clock behind a "Stopping…"
button. The write itself still goes through an openat chain that refuses
symlinks and dirs the seat does not own, and root still creates no directory
in the seat's tree — every refusal is kept, it just no longer exits.

`publish`, `revert` and `remix` (stage 3, SPECS/2026-09-09-apps-serving-gate.md
D6) are the SERVING half: they move directories under /srv/apps-www and clone a
repo under /srv/apps, and their caller is the HOUSE SERVER — the first caller of
this file that is not the broker, and the reason 92-disjorn-apps.sudoers now
names three more aliases. Each one takes ids and NOTHING ELSE: no path, no
program, no unit name, no root. The privileged step is identical in shape to
`run` — validate, then hand a FIXED argv to systemd-run with the seat's
credentials — and the work itself happens in a `<mode>-as-seat` re-entry of this
same file inside that transient unit, as res-appsbuilding. One program, one
place the shapes are checked, and the work runs as the account that owns the
trees it moves. The `-as-seat` modes are not in the sudoers regexes (anchored
`^publish <id>$` and friends), refuse to run as root outright, and give a caller
nothing it did not already have as itself.

    publish <app>    snapshot <www>/<app>/preview/ into the unserved staging
                     root by rsync (the harvest's flags, verbatim), then rotate:
                     live.prev removed, live → live.prev, staged → live. Every
                     step is a rename, so a reader sees the old tree or the new
                     one. Refused if there is no preview, or if a turn of that
                     app is running (a turn's own publish renames preview under
                     the copy).
    revert <app>     swap live and live.prev through a sibling temp name; both
                     must exist. Three renames, no copy.
    remix <p> <c>    git clone --no-local <p> into /srv/apps/<c>, one empty
                     commit "remix of <p>" so lineage is in the log, and the
                     parent's preview copied to the child's by the same
                     staged-rename discipline. Refused if <c> exists.

NOTHING HERE IS POLICY. The session lock, the quota, the token ceiling and the
seat→bot map all live in the broker (slice ii); who may press Live, who owns an
app and who may remix it live in the server. This program decides exactly one
thing: that the identity, the unit name, the argument shapes, the prompt's
location and the resource envelope are the ones the design allows, or nothing
runs.

EXIT CODES
    0   execed (or, under DISJORN_APPS_LAUNCH_DRY_RUN=1, printed the argv)
    64  REFUSED — every refusal below, before any privilege is used. 64 is
        spec §C's number ("Anything else is exit 64 before any privilege is
        used"), and it differs deliberately from disjorn-build-launch's 2 so
        the broker can tell a validation refusal from a systemd failure
        without parsing stderr.
    1   FAILED — a serving operation that was allowed and then broke (rsync,
        git, a rename). Only the `-as-seat` half can produce it; the caller's
        contract is "0 done, 64 refused, anything else failed", and a refusal
        is never a failure.

CHARSETS (spec §C — these are SPEC, not code, because they are the whole wall
between a sudoers-scoped launcher and an arbitrary path):
    resident    ^res-[a-z]{1,24}$
    session     ^[1-9][0-9]{0,8}$
    turn        ^[1-9][0-9]{0,3}$
    app id      ^[a-z2-7]{12}$      (stage 1 D4, base32 lower)
    prompt path realpath must land under that principal's mapped directory

HISTORY: slice (i) admitted one additional literal principal, `keyboard`, so
that the proving turn had a caller in a slice with no broker verb. It was
removed at slice (ii), when `apps-build` landed and every turn has a res-*
seat to be; the sudoers alternative went with it, and spec §C's charset is now
the only one.

The identity is NOT an argument. Every turn of every app runs as
`res-appsbuilding` (spec §C); the <resident> argument names WHOSE PROMPT
DIRECTORY the prompt file must come from, and that is all it does. A caller
that could name the uid could run a turn as anyone.

THE SENTENCE SLICE (ii) STARTS WITH (Claudette #2325, #2329 — three slice-(i)
folds in a row were the same defect): WE KEEP VALIDATING WHERE A THING IS
INSTEAD OF WHAT IT IS. The prompt wall below is built on that: the name is a
cheap first refusal, and every decision is made on the fd — what it is
(regular file, not a FIFO the open would have wedged on), whose it is (the
principal's own uid, never root's), where the kernel actually opened it.
"""

